## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical CAPA design skills: data modeling, lifecycle governance, lightweight APIs, and measurable containment strategies in a multi-tenant, multi-region setup.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model tailored to tenants/regions
- Lifecycle state machine with auditable transitions
- Minimal REST API for creating/updating CAPAs with evidence
- Region/tenant-aware metrics to prove containment
- RCA template and canary remediation plan

## Code Example
```javascript
type CAPA = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  tenants: string[];
  regions: string[];
  evidence: string[];
  batchInfo: {
    schedule: string;
    duration: number;
    resources: string[];
  };
  rootCause: string;
  corrective: string;
  preventive: string;
  owner: string;
  createdAt: Date;
  updatedAt: Date;
};

// Lifecycle states
type CAPAState = 'Identified' | 'Contained' | 'Investigating' | 'Corrected' | 'Verified';

// REST API endpoints
POST /capas
PUT /capas/:id
GET /capas?tenant=:t&region=:r&state=:s
```

## Metrics & Containment
- Tenant/region latency percentiles pre/post containment
- Batch job resource utilization by tenant/region
- Error rates and success rates during remediation
- Canary deployment success metrics by region

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical CAPA design for data segregation issues in multi-tenant exports, focusing on traceability and measurable containment.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model
- Lifecycle state machine
- Minimal REST API design
- Metrics to prove effectiveness
- RCA templates and canary-based remediation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Express route to create CAPA
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
let caps = [];
app.post('/caps', (req,res)=>{ const cap = req.body; cap.id = cap.id || String(Date.now()); caps.push(cap); res.status(201).json(cap);});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you store and attach evidence files?
- How would you enforce tenant isolation in the API and data store?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a CAPA system for data-drift scenarios in a multi-tenant ML/PL workflow, bridging data, observability, and risk controls.

## Key Concepts

- CAPA data model with evidence linkage and drift metrics for traceability
- Lifecycle state machine with clear transitions and SLAs
- REST API design for CAPA creation/update with linked logs/traces
- Tenant-aware metrics to prove containment and avoid recurrence
- RCA template and a staged canary rollback plan for feature-store changes

## Code Example

```javascript
// TypeScript interfaces for CAPA data model
interface CAPA {
  id: string;
  tenant_id: string;
  feature_set: string;
  model_version: string;
  drift_score: { KL: number; KS: number; Wasserstein?: number };
  evidence_logs: string[];
  traces: string[];
  status: 'Detected'|'Investigating'|'Contained'|'Verified'|'Closed';
  created_at: string;
  updated_at: string;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale drift detection for thousands of tenants without increasing false positives?
- How would CAPA decisions be audited and rollback actions validated in production?

